# Creating a Custom REST Service in Dynamics AX using X++

Below is a complete example of how to create a custom REST service in Dynamics AX using X++. This example demonstrates a simple service that retrieves customer data.

## Step 1: Create a Data Contract Class

First, we need to define the data structure that will be exposed by our service.

```xpp
[DataContractAttribute]
class CustomerContract
{
    CustAccount accountNum;
    CustName name;
    CustGroupId custGroup;
    LogisticsPostalAddress address;
}

[DataMemberAttribute('AccountNum')]
public CustAccount parmAccountNum(CustAccount _accountNum = accountNum)
{
    accountNum = _accountNum;
    return accountNum;
}

[DataMemberAttribute('Name')]
public CustName parmName(CustName _name = name)
{
    name = _name;
    return name;
}

[DataMemberAttribute('CustGroup')]
public CustGroupId parmCustGroup(CustGroupId _custGroup = custGroup)
{
    custGroup = _custGroup;
    return custGroup;
}

[DataMemberAttribute('Address')]
public LogisticsPostalAddress parmAddress(LogisticsPostalAddress _address = address)
{
    address = _address;
    return address;
}
```

## Step 2: Create the Service Class

Now, let's create the actual service class that will implement our REST endpoints.

```xpp
[AifCollectionTypeAttribute('return', Types::Class, classStr(CustomerContract))]
class CustomerRestService
{
    public List getCustomers()
    {
        List customerList = new List(Types::Class);
        CustTable custTable;

        // Query to get customer data
        while select * from custTable
        {
            CustomerContract contract = new CustomerContract();
            contract.parmAccountNum(custTable.AccountNum);
            contract.parmName(custTable.name());
            contract.parmCustGroup(custTable.CustGroup);
            contract.parmAddress(custTable.primaryContactInfo().postalAddress());

            customerList.addEnd(contract);
        }

        return customerList;
    }

    public CustomerContract getCustomerById(CustAccount _accountNum)
    {
        CustTable custTable;
        CustomerContract contract;

        select firstOnly * from custTable
            where custTable.AccountNum == _accountNum;

        if (custTable)
        {
            contract = new CustomerContract();
            contract.parmAccountNum(custTable.AccountNum);
            contract.parmName(custTable.name());
            contract.parmCustGroup(custTable.CustGroup);
            contract.parmAddress(custTable.primaryContactInfo().postalAddress());
        }

        return contract;
    }

    public void createCustomer(CustomerContract _customer)
    {
        CustTable custTable;

        ttsBegin;

        custTable.AccountNum = _customer.parmAccountNum();
        custTable.CustGroup = _customer.parmCustGroup();
        custTable.name(_customer.parmName());

        // Set address information
        custTable.primaryContactInfo().postalAddress(_customer.parmAddress());

        custTable.insert();

        ttsCommit;
    }

    public void updateCustomer(CustAccount _accountNum, CustomerContract _customer)
    {
        CustTable custTable;

        ttsBegin;

        select forUpdate * from custTable
            where custTable.AccountNum == _accountNum;

        if (custTable)
        {
            custTable.CustGroup = _customer.parmCustGroup();
            custTable.name(_customer.parmName());
            custTable.primaryContactInfo().postalAddress(_customer.parmAddress());

            custTable.update();
        }

        ttsCommit;
    }

    public void deleteCustomer(CustAccount _accountNum)
    {
        CustTable custTable;

        ttsBegin;

        select forUpdate * from custTable
            where custTable.AccountNum == _accountNum;

        if (custTable)
        {
            custTable.delete();
        }

        ttsCommit;
    }
}
```

## Step 3: Create the Service Controller Class

This class will define the REST endpoints and map them to the service methods.

```xpp
[SysODataCollectionAttribute(classStr(CustomerContract), 'Customers'),
 SysODataActionAttribute('getCustomerById', true),
 SysODataActionAttribute('createCustomer', true),
 SysODataActionAttribute('updateCustomer', true),
 SysODataActionAttribute('deleteCustomer', true)]
class CustomerRestController extends SysODataController
{
    public static CustomerRestController construct()
    {
        return new CustomerRestController();
    }

    public static void main(Args _args)
    {
        CustomerRestController controller = CustomerRestController::construct();
        controller.parmQuery(new SysODataQuery());
        controller.run();
    }

    [SysODataActionAttribute('getCustomers', false)]
    public List getCustomers()
    {
        CustomerRestService service = new CustomerRestService();
        return service.getCustomers();
    }

    [SysODataActionAttribute('getCustomerById', false)]
    public CustomerContract getCustomerById(CustAccount _accountNum)
    {
        CustomerRestService service = new CustomerRestService();
        return service.getCustomerById(_accountNum);
    }

    [SysODataActionAttribute('createCustomer', false)]
    public void createCustomer(CustomerContract _customer)
    {
        CustomerRestService service = new CustomerRestService();
        service.createCustomer(_customer);
    }

    [SysODataActionAttribute('updateCustomer', false)]
    public void updateCustomer(CustAccount _accountNum, CustomerContract _customer)
    {
        CustomerRestService service = new CustomerRestService();
        service.updateCustomer(_accountNum, _customer);
    }

    [SysODataActionAttribute('deleteCustomer', false)]
    public void deleteCustomer(CustAccount _accountNum)
    {
        CustomerRestService service = new CustomerRestService();
        service.deleteCustomer(_accountNum);
    }
}
```

## Step 4: Deploy the Service

To deploy the service, you need to:

1. Create a new service group in the AOT under Services > Service Groups
2. Add your service to the group
3. Deploy the service group

Here's a job to deploy the service:

```xpp
static void deployCustomerRestService(Args _args)
{
    // Create a new service group
    AifServiceGroup serviceGroup = AifServiceGroup::construct();
    serviceGroup.parmName("CustomerServices");
    serviceGroup.parmDescription("Customer REST Services");
    serviceGroup.save();

    // Add the service to the group
    AifService service = AifService::findByClassName(classStr(CustomerRestController));
    serviceGroup.addService(service);

    // Deploy the service group
    serviceGroup.deploy();
}
```

## Testing the Service

After deployment, you can test the service using URLs like:

- `GET /api/services/CustomerServices/CustomerRestController/Customers` - Get all customers
- `GET /api/services/CustomerServices/CustomerRestController/getCustomerById?accountNum=1001` - Get specific customer
- `POST /api/services/CustomerServices/CustomerRestController/createCustomer` - Create new customer
- `PUT /api/services/CustomerServices/CustomerRestController/updateCustomer?accountNum=1001` - Update customer
- `DELETE /api/services/CustomerServices/CustomerRestController/deleteCustomer?accountNum=1001` - Delete customer

## Notes

1. Make sure to add proper error handling in production code.
2. Consider adding authentication and authorization checks.
3. For complex scenarios, you might need to implement additional methods for validation and business logic.
4. The service will be available at `/api/services/[ServiceGroupName]/[ControllerName]/[MethodName]`

